 [17592186332569 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535126 true] [17592186332569 1111 #uuid "a454308d-8289-5655-9cc0-497565c7d239" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332570 1034 "x\\le 1" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535126 true] [17592186332570 1111 #uuid "adb4e4e4-430a-57fb-93fd-707fbbe1e353" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332571 1034 "x\\le -2" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535126 true] [17592186332571 1111 #uuid "5626e6f4-2621-5391-a65b-da14e54a1bd7" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332572 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332573 13194139535126 true] [17592186332572 1089 "q-130620" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332572 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535126 true] [17592186332572 1100 "Find the possible values of the constant $x$ given that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ x & -1 & -x\\end{aligned}]$ is singular." 13194139535126 true] [17592186332572 1113 #uuid "2d10f730-d88e-5867-be87-5a616a4cee43" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332572 1145 "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ x & -1 & -x\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -x\\end{aligned}|-5|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ x & -x\\end{aligned}|+8|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ x & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(-x+2)-5(-x-2x)+8(-1-x) \\\\  & =-3x+6+15x-8-8x \\\\  & =4x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332574 13194139535126 true] [17592186332573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332575 13194139535126 true] [17592186332573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332576 13194139535126 true] [17592186332573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332577 13194139535126 true] [17592186332573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332578 13194139535126 true] [17592186332573 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535126 true] [17592186332573 1041 "selection" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332573 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332576 13194139535126 true] [17592186332573 1110 #uuid "acffd64e-aa01-595f-a2a3-489a6b759b61" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332574 1034 "-9" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535126 true] [17592186332574 1111 #uuid "e529cc70-407c-58e6-8d72-98df69c51ed0" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332575 1034 "\\frac{7}{4}" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535126 true] [17592186332575 1111 #uuid "3f4bc631-772a-5623-aeea-3baa1881ddd5" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332576 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535126 true] [17592186332576 1111 #uuid "cd19d113-9bdf-54fb-9de8-b7c25d11c33b" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332577 1034 "8" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535126 true] [17592186332577 1111 #uuid "e3a1db8f-8d05-5677-b494-1c75207587c1" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332578 1034 "5" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332578 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535126 true] [17592186332578 1111 #uuid "9778c2d8-4e24-566a-959f-c9946b50f0e4" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332579 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332580 13194139535126 true] [17592186332579 1089 "q-141101" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332579 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535126 true] [17592186332579 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{5}$\n- $y={x}^{10}$\n- $y=5{x}^{4}$" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332579 1113 #uuid "a3cfc1fb-f2ea-5d4f-9851-aafa6955258e" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332579 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{5},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{5})}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & =5{x}^{4}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{5}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{10},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=10{x}^{9}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{10})}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}5{x}^{9} \\\\ 5{x}^{9} & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{10}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=5{x}^{4},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=20{x}^{3}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5(5{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}25{x}^{3} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 5{x}^{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=5{x}^{4}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332581 13194139535126 true] [17592186332580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332582 13194139535126 true] [17592186332580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332583 13194139535126 true] [17592186332580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332584 13194139535126 true] [17592186332580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332585 13194139535126 true] [17592186332580 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535126 true] [17592186332580 1041 "selection" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332580 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332582 13194139535126 true] [17592186332580 1110 #uuid "1b2aa5b5-7356-59c9-b5dc-04c7139ccd54" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332581 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535126 true] [17592186332581 1111 #uuid "fa54bca0-f61d-569d-ac43-3edfc933e221" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332582 1034 "I only" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535126 true] [17592186332582 1111 #uuid "78d10572-47e8-5f06-9377-4158762acd1d" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332583 1034 "III only" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535126 true] [17592186332583 1111 #uuid "e1ac4cf4-18c9-5e56-afe3-a201adc55a3b" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332584 1034 "None" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535126 true] [17592186332584 1111 #uuid "72671062-989c-5748-ae57-796cb7b8a96e" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332585 1034 "II only" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535126 true] [17592186332585 1111 #uuid "d988ef17-7cd0-57cf-bedd-df476651cd7d" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332587 13194139535126 true] [17592186332586 1089 "q-162307" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332586 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535126 true] [17592186332586 1100 "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $5$th roots of unity?" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332586 1113 #uuid "b1b3a339-5cbb-547a-ab0a-fcc002181195" 13194139535126 true] [17592186332586 1145 "Geometrically, the $5$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular pentagon (regular $5$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Aca